# an-undefended-base — plan 12 B7, the CONTROL.
#
# A core, a spawn, and nothing whatsoever in between.  Three waves walk
# in and stand on the core until the wallet runs out.  It is the number
# every other scenario in this phase is measured against: a base that
# falls in 61 ticks and a base that falls in 104 are the difference
# between "damage exists" and "defences work", and only the second is
# worth building.
#
# ── Why the control is not "the same map with the walls deleted" ──
#
# It nearly is, and deliberately: `a-defended-base.keys` is this file
# plus five `click`s.  `plans/12` § Invariant gate names the failure —
# *equal times = the scenario measures nothing, whatever it draws* — so
# the two runs have to differ in the defences and in NOTHING else.  Same
# ground, same core, same spawn, same wave list, same seed of a walk.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   five rows of grass, sixteen columns:  (-3, -2) .. (12, 2)
#   core at (0, 0), spawn at (11, 0) facing west
#
# ⚠ **Five rows is now load-bearing where it used to be scenery.**
# Before plan 11 F7b the attack was a COLUMN however much room it had,
# so the clock read 161 over five rows and 161 over thirteen.  Now a
# blocked enemy steps beside, and the band has to be wide enough for
# the wave to fan across the core's own footprint — which is a radius-1
# disc, so five rows is exactly enough and no more.

echo an undefended base: nothing in the way, and the wallet is the clock

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, eleven hexes east — clear of close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10) and well inside the scrambler bubble (25).
# ⚠ TWICE more: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower ->
# spawn since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# ── The waves: `numbers.json` § wave_system, first two ──────────
#
# 5 and 8 off the authored `wave_list`, spaced by the file's own
# `inter_wave_delay` of 15 s — which is 22.5 ticks, so 22.  Nothing
# here invents a number.
#
# ⚠ Two waves rather than the list's three, for the gate's time
# budget.  When this file was written a third wave of twelve changed
# the clock by NOTHING — the queue capped the drain at two nibblers
# whatever the roster held.  Plan 11 F7b ended that, so a third wave
# WOULD now shorten it: thirteen robots already fill the seven-hex
# footprint, and more of them just refill it faster as the front ones
# are relieved.
wave 5
count alive 5 5
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

# ── The clock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ **61 ticks, and it used to be 161.**  Plan 11 F7b is the whole of
# that difference.  Until it shipped, a wave arriving down one axis
# QUEUED: the field offers exactly one closer neighbour on an axis, and
# F5c had a blocked enemy wait rather than step beside it — so thirteen
# robots reached this core and exactly TWO ever drained the wallet, at
# a flat 2 pt/s, however many were behind them.
#
# They now fill the core's footprint: centre plus six neighbours, which
# is `numbers.json` § core.footprint_layout and the ceiling by
# construction.  Seven nibblers is 4.67 pt/s, so an undefended base
# falls in a third of the time and **the drain finally scales with the
# wave**.  `tests/11_f7b_the_sidestep.loft` prices it directly.
fall 800
ticks 64 74
wallet 0.0 0.0

# Nobody laid a finger on them.  This is the control's defining
# property and the reason `count alive` is here rather than in a
# comment: the moment something in this scenario kills an enemy, it is
# no longer the undefended base.
count alive 13 13

snap b7_an_undefended_base
